WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.269 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.370
-Hey, everybody.
Welcome back.

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:05.404
If it is alright with you,

00:00:05.404 --> 00:00:06.906
I'm just gonna jump
right into it.

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:09.742
We talked about how
Justin Bieber

00:00:09.742 --> 00:00:12.245
was gonna have his own

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:14.647
Justin Bieber-inspired
doughnut holes

00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:17.317
at Tim Horton's,
the Canadian doughnut shop.

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:22.321
I was told by many
of our Canadian viewers

00:00:22.321 --> 00:00:24.724
that in Canada, they are not
called doughnut holes.

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:27.727
They are called Timbits.

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:31.230
Interesting --
Timbit has another meaning.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.899
It is a tidbit

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:35.401
not interesting enough
to share.

00:00:35.401 --> 00:00:36.903
[ Laughter ]

00:00:39.906 --> 00:00:43.576
Aisling Bea, wonderful actress,
was here.

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:45.911
And she talked about how

00:00:45.911 --> 00:00:48.914
Dustin the Turkey,
a turkey puppet,

00:00:48.914 --> 00:00:51.784
actually was the entry
for Ireland

00:00:51.784 --> 00:00:54.954
in the Eurovision Song Contest
in 2008.

00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:56.422
I said, "How did he do?
Did he win?"

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:57.757
She said, "No,
he was eliminated."

00:00:57.757 --> 00:01:01.427
And I joked,
"Well, I'm sure he finished --

00:01:01.427 --> 00:01:04.264
he was the top-finishing
turkey."

00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:06.266
He was not.

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:07.967
Turkey was.

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:10.970
[ Laughter ]

00:01:14.273 --> 00:01:17.443
We had a joke in yesterday's
monologue about a manger.

00:01:17.443 --> 00:01:19.278
We referred to a manger
as if it was a building.

00:01:19.278 --> 00:01:22.782
A manger is not a building.
It is a trough.

00:01:22.782 --> 00:01:24.450
Where they feed animals.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.286
And, interesting --
I did some research --

00:01:27.286 --> 00:01:29.289
They came up with
the word manger

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:32.792
because trough
didn't sound good in carols.

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:35.027
[ Laughter ]

00:01:35.027 --> 00:01:37.363
Like, when it was like,
"Away in a trough,"

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:39.566
like immediately,
people were like, "Ugh."

00:01:44.136 --> 00:01:47.473
Talked about I was feeling bad,
and when I feel bad,

00:01:47.473 --> 00:01:49.809
my friend Andy Samberg
has a homeopathic cure.

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:53.145
And I called him up and he did
his Robert Smith impression.

00:01:53.145 --> 00:01:56.816
A lot of people said
that's not --

00:01:56.816 --> 00:01:59.718
"What you described is not
homeopathic medicine."

00:01:59.718 --> 00:02:01.954
The way homeopathic
medicine works

00:02:01.954 --> 00:02:03.456
is it doesn't.

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:05.592
[ Laughter ]

00:02:09.795 --> 00:02:12.464
I guess you dilute --
you need to dilute

00:02:12.464 --> 00:02:13.933
the thing that's
making you sick.

00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:17.270
You take a very small
dose of that

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:20.272
to supposedly make you better.

00:02:20.272 --> 00:02:21.941
I will say --

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:23.442
I love him --

00:02:23.442 --> 00:02:25.945
Andy's Robert Smith impression

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:28.114
was pretty diluted.

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:29.615
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.615 --> 00:02:31.451
I've tried to get him...

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:33.285
Hate to say it --
I've tried to get him to go

00:02:33.285 --> 00:02:35.388
to impression camp.

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:37.223
He won't go.

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:39.592
And it only works
if he wants to go.

00:02:42.394 --> 00:02:43.596
Talked about --

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:45.297
Again, I think this is week four
of talking about

00:02:45.297 --> 00:02:47.433
"Shoemaker and the Elves."

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:51.370
And at first, I was told --
I referred to it as a fable.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.874
"Shoemaker and the Elves"
is the story about the elves

00:02:54.874 --> 00:02:57.210
who are paid
to write jokes here.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.544
When they do a bad job,

00:02:58.544 --> 00:03:00.879
Shoemaker throws them
out the window.

00:03:00.879 --> 00:03:03.682
And I called it a fable,
and people said no,

00:03:03.682 --> 00:03:05.818
a fable needs a moral,
and I said the moral is

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:07.753
don't mess with Mike Shoemaker.

00:03:07.753 --> 00:03:09.088
And then another person said,

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:11.123
"Oh, fables have to
have animals,

00:03:11.123 --> 00:03:12.458
and elves aren't animals."

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:15.461
But I do realize
where I made my mistake,

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:18.631
which is when Shoemaker squeezes
them out the window,

00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:22.135
he does say, "Enjoy your flight,
ya [bleep] animal!"

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:24.204
[ Laughter ]

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:31.978
You know, sometimes history
creeps its way into Corrections,

00:03:31.978 --> 00:03:36.949
and we learned a lot
about defenestration,

00:03:36.949 --> 00:03:40.119
which is the act of throwing
somebody out a window.

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:42.388
And it happens a lot in Prague.

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:46.558
The most famous ones
happen in Prague.

00:03:46.558 --> 00:03:49.562
If you look up defenestrations,
it's gonna take you

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:51.397
right to the defenestrations
of Prague.

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:54.100
There are three main
defenestrations in Prague --

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.736
1419, 1483, 1618.

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:59.572
Big gap between the last two.

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:03.109
They went from 1483 to 1618
without a defenestration.

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:04.944
People probably --

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:08.281
at that point,
probably thought they were safe.

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:11.783
That the crime wave
of defenestration was over.

00:04:11.783 --> 00:04:13.285
Then next thing you know,

00:04:13.285 --> 00:04:15.454
go to the tower of your castle
and just chucked out,

00:04:15.454 --> 00:04:16.856
just --
[ Laughs ]

00:04:16.856 --> 00:04:19.692
...just DJ Jazzy Jeff'ed
out the window.

00:04:19.692 --> 00:04:21.694
[ Laughter ]

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:25.365
But this is
the fascinating thing.

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:27.700
The third of the defenestrations

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:31.704
is known as the Second
Defenestration of Prague,

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:35.374
because I guess the second one,
the one that happened in 1483,

00:04:35.374 --> 00:04:39.044
is not considered
a significant defenestration.

00:04:39.044 --> 00:04:41.914
Must have been a real blow
to the families...

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:44.750
[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:45.918
...who got chucked
out the window

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:49.021
for the second defenestration.

00:04:49.021 --> 00:04:51.858
When the Prague cops showed up
to the house,

00:04:51.858 --> 00:04:56.529
told them, you know, "Your loved
one was tossed out a window,"

00:04:56.529 --> 00:04:58.698
and they said, "Well...

00:04:58.698 --> 00:05:00.700
at least he'll always
be remembered

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:03.535
as being a victim of the second
defenestration of Prague."

00:05:03.535 --> 00:05:05.371
And the cops were like,
"Ehhh..."

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:07.205
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.205 --> 00:05:09.409
"We might save the name."

00:05:11.778 --> 00:05:13.946
I also said it started
the Thirty Year War.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:15.280
It's the Thirty Years' War.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:16.315
The Thirty Year War --

00:05:16.315 --> 00:05:18.017
8 1/2 months.

00:05:22.654 --> 00:05:26.158
We mentioned seagulls.

00:05:26.158 --> 00:05:28.660
And we showed a photo of this.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.964
And a couple people said,
"That is not a seagull.

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:34.467
That is a silver gull."

00:05:34.467 --> 00:05:39.305
And obviously,
I was pretty upset

00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:41.307
that Graphics would
do me like that.

00:05:41.307 --> 00:05:44.643
Feels like it'd be pretty easy
to find a photo of a seagull

00:05:44.643 --> 00:05:49.482
instead of a very rare
silver gull from Australia.

00:05:49.482 --> 00:05:52.985
And you know what you call me

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:57.490
when I think Graphics
is gonna give me the right bird?

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:59.492
Gullible.

00:05:59.492 --> 00:06:01.160
Baze.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:02.862
[ Laughter ]

00:06:05.164 --> 00:06:07.667
Why'd you tell me...

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:09.369
that would work?

00:06:14.841 --> 00:06:17.009
We talked about
the Attenborough brothers.

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:19.011
I thought they were the same.

00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:21.681
Richard and David.

00:06:21.681 --> 00:06:23.549
David, the naturalist;
Richard --

00:06:23.549 --> 00:06:24.616
I referred to Richard the actor.

00:06:24.616 --> 00:06:25.951
He was in "Jurassic Park."

00:06:25.951 --> 00:06:29.622
A lot of people said I
overlooked his directing career.

00:06:29.622 --> 00:06:31.624
And I apologize
to Richard Attenborough.

00:06:31.624 --> 00:06:34.626
"Gandhi."

00:06:34.626 --> 00:06:36.295
"Chaplin."

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:39.132
"A Chorus Line."

00:06:39.132 --> 00:06:41.634
That one's a bridge too far.

00:06:41.634 --> 00:06:43.168
[ Laughter ]

00:06:43.168 --> 00:06:44.337
Did you get it?

00:06:44.337 --> 00:06:46.339
[ Sing-song voice ]
I really hope you get it.

00:06:46.339 --> 00:06:49.341
[ Laughter ]

00:06:49.341 --> 00:06:51.177
I said that --
[ Laughs ]

00:06:51.177 --> 00:06:54.513
I basically said there was some
tension between the brothers

00:06:54.513 --> 00:06:57.450
because, you know, David did
all these documentaries,

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:58.784
and does all these
documentaries,

00:06:58.784 --> 00:07:01.320
about the modern natural world,
and then, you know,

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.723
Richard got this big part
in "Jurassic Park"

00:07:03.723 --> 00:07:06.225
and wouldn't let David,
you know, help with it,

00:07:06.225 --> 00:07:07.660
because it's about dinosaurs.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.062
But then people said, "Yeah,
but actually, David has done,

00:07:10.062 --> 00:07:14.200
you know, multiple documentaries
about dinosaurs, as well."

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:16.869
So it was probably
even more awkward,

00:07:16.869 --> 00:07:17.903
you know,
when Richard was like --

00:07:17.903 --> 00:07:20.005
[ Cockney accent ]
"Oi, David!

00:07:20.005 --> 00:07:22.842
Doin' a new Spielberg.

00:07:22.842 --> 00:07:25.744
Yeah, got a part in the new
Spielberg, don't I, David?

00:07:25.744 --> 00:07:27.546
It's about dinosaurs."
And David was like --

00:07:27.546 --> 00:07:32.051
[ Cockney accent] "Oh, Dickie, I
know a fair bit about dinosaurs.

00:07:32.051 --> 00:07:34.386
You wanna put in a good word
for me?

00:07:34.386 --> 00:07:36.222
Would love to work
on the new Spielberg."

00:07:36.222 --> 00:07:37.723
And he's like,
"Oh, I'm sorry, David.

00:07:37.723 --> 00:07:39.392
It's a blockbuster, innit?"

00:07:39.392 --> 00:07:40.392
[ Laughter ]

00:07:40.392 --> 00:07:42.694
"Yeah, it's a blockbuster.

00:07:42.694 --> 00:07:46.999
Nah, Steve thinks your
documentaries are a bit slow."

00:07:46.999 --> 00:07:49.001
[ Laughter ]

00:07:49.001 --> 00:07:50.336
[Normal voice ]
Then their brother John came in.

00:07:50.336 --> 00:07:51.537
He's like, "Oi!"

00:07:51.537 --> 00:07:53.939
Now, here's the thing,
for the rest of this bit.

00:07:53.939 --> 00:07:56.275
For the rest of this bit,
you need to know a key fact.

00:07:56.275 --> 00:07:59.111
There were three
Attenborough brothers.

00:07:59.111 --> 00:08:02.949
The ones we've been talking
about -- Richard, David --

00:08:02.949 --> 00:08:04.616
knighted.

00:08:04.616 --> 00:08:06.785
John -- not knighted.

00:08:06.785 --> 00:08:08.988
[ Cockney accent ]
"Oi! Dickie! David!

00:08:08.988 --> 00:08:09.988
Supper's ready."

00:08:09.988 --> 00:08:12.992
"Oi! What'd you call us?!

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:17.830
I think there's a word missin'
in front of our name, John."

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:19.298
"Am I really gonna
have to do this?"

00:08:19.298 --> 00:08:22.301
"That's right, you are."

00:08:22.301 --> 00:08:27.240
"Sir Dickie, Sir David,
dinner's ready."

00:08:29.475 --> 00:08:31.143
[ Laughter ]

00:08:34.446 --> 00:08:36.449
[ Normal voice ] I guess people
said, "You know, actually" --

00:08:36.449 --> 00:08:40.786
And again, I know
that's not how they talk.

00:08:40.786 --> 00:08:42.788
They went to a boys school
in Leicester,

00:08:42.788 --> 00:08:44.456
and someone said
they should actually have

00:08:44.456 --> 00:08:46.925
a Leicester accent, but um...

00:08:46.925 --> 00:08:48.927
I would hate to see Seth Meyers
try a Leicester accent."

00:08:48.927 --> 00:08:50.962
'Cause I guess it's a hard --

00:08:50.962 --> 00:08:53.132
in their head, they perceive it
as a hard accent.

00:08:53.132 --> 00:08:55.334
I do a very good
Leicester accent.

00:08:57.803 --> 00:09:00.473
[ As Lester Holt ]
Welcome to "NBC Nightly News."

00:09:00.473 --> 00:09:02.575
[ Laughter ]

00:09:10.816 --> 00:09:13.486
Whoever's from Leicester
is gonna love that one.

00:09:13.486 --> 00:09:16.655
[ Laughter ]

00:09:16.655 --> 00:09:19.158
I confused windmills
and wind turbines.

00:09:19.158 --> 00:09:22.161
A lot of you were angry
about that.

00:09:22.161 --> 00:09:23.563
Um...

00:09:25.665 --> 00:09:28.834
If I was -- If I was building
windmills or wind turbines,

00:09:28.834 --> 00:09:30.336
I would build it next
to a jackal's house

00:09:30.336 --> 00:09:32.338
'cause you guys blow so hard.

00:09:32.338 --> 00:09:34.840
[ Laughter ]

00:09:43.849 --> 00:09:45.217
Alright.

00:09:48.086 --> 00:09:51.089
Someone sent a comment --

00:09:51.089 --> 00:09:53.759
And, you know, again,

00:09:53.759 --> 00:09:56.161
we've established that, uh...

00:09:56.161 --> 00:09:57.997
who you are, jackals,

00:09:57.997 --> 00:10:01.334
but I want to finish
with this comment.

00:10:01.334 --> 00:10:04.503
"A few years ago, I went to an
audience with David Attenborough

00:10:04.503 --> 00:10:07.640
in Australia, and the audience
were able to put in questions.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:09.308
My friend's question was picked,
and he asked,

00:10:09.308 --> 00:10:10.475
'If you could be
in Jurassic Park,

00:10:10.475 --> 00:10:11.643
what would you want
to see first?'

00:10:11.643 --> 00:10:13.579
And he said,
'The first thing would be

00:10:13.579 --> 00:10:15.747
to go see my brother Richard.'

00:10:15.747 --> 00:10:18.150
This was after Richard
had passed away.

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:21.287
Just a lovely, lovely,
wonderful man."

00:10:23.155 --> 00:10:25.658
Every time I think

00:10:25.658 --> 00:10:29.828
the only way
jackals can make me feel

00:10:29.828 --> 00:10:32.664
is frustrated,

00:10:32.664 --> 00:10:35.333
you write something beautiful
like that.

00:10:35.333 --> 00:10:38.503
Thank you for sharing it.

00:10:38.503 --> 00:10:43.675
This has been Corrections.

00:10:43.675 --> 00:10:45.277
See me next week.

